---
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../consts';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { url } from '../url';

const LINKEDIN_URL = 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/brian-fotheringham';
---

<Page
	title="About"
	description="Brian Fotheringham: data and AI platform architect at GE Aerospace, working on governed data for people and agents."
>
	<article class="wrap">
		<header class="head">
			<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
			<h1>About</h1>
		</header>
		<div class="prose">
			<p>
				I'm Brian Fotheringham. I'm a data and AI platform architect at GE Aerospace, based in
				Cleveland. I work on the part of the stack where data meets AI: getting data to people and
				agents quickly, while keeping clear who owns it, who can use it, and why.
			</p>
			<p>
				My working style is simple. Set a standard, enforce it with tooling instead of memory, roll it
				out crawl-walk-run, and change it when reality pushes back. I like cloud primitives, small
				versioned config files, and systems that fail closed.
			</p>

			<h2>Now</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Staff Data &amp; Information Architect, GE Aerospace</strong> (2024 to present). I'm the
				second most senior architect on the Data Fabric team, which is modernizing how the company
				stores, governs and shares data for both people and AI, across commercial AWS and GovCloud.
				Most of my job is technical direction: setting architecture and standards and leading the
				teams that deliver them.
			</p>
			<p>Some of what I've built there:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A commercial lakehouse on Apache Iceberg.</strong> Data from a higher-regulated zone
					now lands once, on S3 with Lake Formation and Glue, and Palantir, Databricks and AWS all read
					that one copy instead of making their own.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The Databricks platform.</strong> I took it from a 100-day trial to an enterprise
					platform, with workspaces and Unity Catalog organized per data domain so each domain owns its
					data, and data produced in Databricks shared back to the central catalog for every other
					platform.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A cloud-native ingestion service.</strong> A config-driven, self-service control plane
					on AWS primitives (ECS and Batch) that replaced a licensed ingestion product at a fraction of
					the cost. It now handles hundreds of sources, structured and unstructured.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Passport, our data contract primitive.</strong> Every dataset declares its owner,
					schema, SLAs, quality checks and regulatory tags, and the contract is enforced in CI/CD.
					Those contracts became the attributes our access control decides on.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Enterprise attribute-based access control.</strong> I co-designed the policy
					framework, the resource store (built from Passport contracts), and enforcement through MCP
					servers, so access follows written policy instead of one-off grants.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>MCP Factory.</strong> I lead the enterprise MCP program: a shared package with
					identity, policy enforcement, audit and observability built in, a team of forward-deployed
					engineers building servers to that standard, and the security review standards our cyber
					team now applies to every MCP.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Agent publishing platform.</strong> I helped design and build the platform where
					teams publish and run agents, and I'm leading the standard for how agents carry ownership,
					audit and change management.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Before that</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Senior Technical Product Manager, GE Aerospace</strong> (2022 to 2024). I owned a 100
				TB data lake on Spark, Glue and EMR serving more than 1,000 analysts and engineers, and built
				an event plane that let downstream teams act on new data as it landed.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Digital Technology Leadership Program, GE Aviation</strong> (2020 to 2022). I
				architected a Redshift warehouse for an Italian subsidiary under Italian and US compliance
				requirements, and built a convolutional neural network for engine test analytics.
			</p>

			<h2>Open source and writing</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/FatherBrian/trellis"><strong>Trellis and Reef</strong></a>:
					open-source tools that let an AI agent act for a person only within a task's scope. Every
					tool call passes Cedar policy checks and lands in a hash-chained audit ledger.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href={url('/blog/primitives-for-agents/')}><strong>Primitives for agents</strong></a>:
					why agents need small, versioned, tool-enforced config files, and the first one,
					<code>reef.lock</code>, which pins MCP tool definitions so a changed tool can't slip through.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href={url('/lab/')}><strong>The Lab</strong></a>: live demos of the ideas I write about.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Education</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>M.S. Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Georgia Institute of Technology (2026)</li>
				<li>B.S. Software Engineering and B.S. Information Systems, Miami University (2020)</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
			<p>
				<a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a> · <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a> ·
				<a href={url('/rss.xml')}>RSS</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</article>
</Page>

<style>
	.head {
		max-width: var(--measure);
		margin: 4.5rem auto 2rem;
	}
	.head .eyebrow {
		display: none;
	}
	@media (max-width: 640px) {
		.head {
			margin-top: 2.75rem;
		}
	}
</style>
